      "modelAnswer": "Working from home, once a rare privilege, has become a standard arrangement for millions of employees across numerous industries. While remote working creates some genuine challenges, I believe its advantages are considerably more significant.\n\nThe foremost benefit is the improvement in work-life balance and productivity. Without the need to commute, employees reclaim hours each day that can be devoted to family, exercise or rest, which reduces stress and boosts overall wellbeing. A software developer, for example, can begin work refreshed rather than exhausted after an hour stuck in traffic. Companies also gain, as they can reduce expensive office space and recruit talented staff from anywhere in the world, no longer limited by geography. This flexibility frequently translates into higher motivation and lower staff turnover.\n\nNonetheless, remote working has clear disadvantages. Many people feel isolated when separated from colleagues, and the loss of casual interaction can harm both mental health and creative collaboration. Spontaneous brainstorming, which often sparks innovation, is harder to achieve through video calls. Furthermore, the boundary between professional and personal life can blur, causing some employees to overwork because they never truly leave the office. Weaker supervision may also reduce accountability for less motivated individuals.\n\nHowever, I would contend that these problems can be mitigated effectively. Hybrid models, in which staff attend the office a few days each week, preserve social bonds and teamwork while retaining the flexibility of home working. With clear schedules and the right technology, isolation and overwork can be largely controlled.\n\nIn conclusion, although remote working risks isolation and blurred boundaries, the gains in flexibility, wellbeing and cost efficiency outweigh these drawbacks, particularly when balanced sensibly with some time spent in a shared workplace."
