      "topic": "Urbanization",
      "prompt": "Across the developing world, cities are expanding at an extraordinary pace as millions of people move from the countryside in search of better lives. Do the advantages of this rapid urbanization outweigh the disadvantages?",
      "modelAnswer": "Rapid urbanization is one of the defining features of the modern developing world, as enormous numbers of rural inhabitants migrate to cities each year. Although this process generates serious problems, I believe its benefits ultimately outweigh its drawbacks.\n\nThe principal advantage of urbanization is economic opportunity. Cities concentrate industries, services and investment, creating jobs and higher wages that rural areas simply cannot offer. A young person moving from a farming village to a metropolis such as Lagos or Mumbai can access factory work, education and healthcare that would otherwise be unavailable. This concentration of people and activity also drives innovation, since the exchange of ideas in dense urban centres tends to accelerate technological and cultural progress, lifting living standards for society as a whole.\n\nHowever, the disadvantages of unchecked growth are severe. When cities expand faster than their infrastructure, the result is overcrowding, traffic congestion, pollution and sprawling slums that lack clean water and sanitation. Millions of new arrivals in cities like Dhaka, for example, live in informal settlements with inadequate services, which fuels disease and crime. Such rapid expansion also places immense strain on the environment, consuming land and resources at an unsustainable rate.\n\nDespite these challenges, I maintain that the difficulties stem largely from poor planning rather than urbanization itself. With sustained investment in housing, public transport and utilities, governments can absorb migration successfully, as several Asian cities have demonstrated. The economic dynamism that cities provide remains the surest route out of poverty for developing nations.\n\nIn conclusion, while rapid urbanization brings overcrowding and environmental strain, its capacity to generate opportunity, innovation and prosperity means that, with proper management, the advantages clearly outweigh the disadvantages."
