      "modelAnswer": "English has emerged as the world's leading language for trade, research and diplomacy, functioning as a common tongue between people of different nationalities. Although this dominance carries certain risks, I am convinced that the advantages of a shared global language outweigh the disadvantages.\n\nThe greatest benefit is the ease of communication it provides. When a German engineer, a Japanese scientist and a Brazilian businessperson can all converse in English, international cooperation becomes vastly simpler, accelerating trade, scientific discovery and the spread of knowledge. Academic journals and the internet are dominated by English, meaning that anyone who masters it gains access to an immense pool of information. This shared medium also promotes mutual understanding between cultures and helps resolve conflicts through dialogue rather than misunderstanding.\n\nNevertheless, the rise of English poses a genuine threat to linguistic diversity. As smaller languages are neglected in favour of English, many risk extinction, and with them the unique cultural heritage and ways of thinking they embody. There is also an issue of fairness, since native English speakers enjoy a natural advantage in global business and education, while others must invest years of effort to compete on equal terms, creating an uneven playing field.\n\nWhile these concerns are legitimate, I would argue that they do not outweigh the practical gains. Speaking English need not mean abandoning one's mother tongue; bilingualism allows people to enjoy global connectivity while preserving their heritage, and many governments now actively protect minority languages.\n\nIn conclusion, although a global language threatens smaller tongues and advantages native speakers, the immense benefits of seamless international communication and shared knowledge make the advantages of English as a lingua franca clearly outweigh its disadvantages."
