      "topic": "Transport",
      "prompt": "In many cities around the world, traffic congestion has become an increasingly serious problem, causing delays, frustration and economic losses. What are the causes of this problem and what measures could be taken to solve it?",
      "modelAnswer": "Gridlocked roads have become a defining feature of modern urban life, costing economies billions in lost productivity and harming residents' wellbeing. This essay will examine the main causes of traffic congestion before proposing realistic solutions.\n\nThe primary cause is the rapid rise in private car ownership, which has outpaced the expansion of road infrastructure. As incomes grow, families increasingly view owning a car as a symbol of status and a guarantee of convenience, so the number of vehicles entering city centres each morning rises relentlessly. A second cause is poor urban planning, particularly the concentration of jobs in central business districts far from residential suburbs. In Bangkok, for instance, millions commute long distances daily because affordable housing exists only on the city's outskirts, channelling everyone onto the same congested arteries at peak hours.\n\nSeveral measures could ease this pressure. Most importantly, governments should invest heavily in efficient public transport, such as underground metro lines and dedicated bus lanes, making them faster and cheaper than driving. London's congestion charge demonstrates how pricing access to the city centre can dramatically cut traffic while funding better buses. Additionally, authorities could promote remote working and stagger office hours so that commuters do not all travel simultaneously, smoothing demand throughout the day. Encouraging cycling through protected lanes, as Copenhagen has done successfully, would further reduce reliance on cars for short journeys.\n\nIn conclusion, traffic congestion stems largely from soaring car ownership and the separation of homes from workplaces. Through substantial investment in public transport, congestion pricing and flexible working arrangements, cities can realistically reduce gridlock and create more liveable environments for their inhabitants."
