      "modelAnswer": "Pollution of the air we breathe and the water we drink has emerged as one of the gravest threats facing the modern world, endangering public health and destroying fragile ecosystems. This essay will explore the principal causes of this crisis and recommend effective remedies.\n\nThe foremost cause is unchecked industrial activity. Factories, particularly in rapidly developing economies, frequently discharge toxic chemicals into rivers and pump smoke into the atmosphere to maximise profits while avoiding the cost of filtration equipment. A clear example is the heavy contamination of the Citarum River in Indonesia, where textile plants release dyes and heavy metals that have rendered the water lethal to aquatic life. A further cause is society's dependence on fossil fuels for transport and electricity, which generates enormous quantities of particulate matter and greenhouse gases, blanketing cities such as Delhi in hazardous smog each winter.\n\nThe most effective solution is for governments to impose and rigorously enforce strict environmental regulations, accompanied by heavy fines for violators. When polluting becomes financially painful, companies invest in cleaner technologies and waste-treatment systems. Equally important is accelerating the transition to renewable energy sources such as solar and wind power, which produce electricity without poisoning the air. Countries like Denmark have shown that wind farms can supply a substantial share of national power demand. Finally, public awareness campaigns can encourage citizens to use public transport and reduce consumption, lowering the overall pollution burden.\n\nIn conclusion, irresponsible industry and reliance on fossil fuels are the chief culprits behind dangerous pollution. By enforcing tough laws, embracing renewable energy and educating the public, nations can protect both human health and the natural world for future generations."
