      "modelAnswer": "Rising obesity has become a public health emergency across much of the developed and developing world, straining healthcare systems and reducing life expectancy. This essay will analyse why waistlines are expanding and suggest practical measures to reverse the trend.\n\nA central cause is the modern diet, which is dominated by cheap, calorie-dense processed foods. Fast food and sugary drinks are aggressively marketed, conveniently available and often cheaper than fresh produce, so families on tight budgets gravitate towards them. In Mexico, for example, soaring soft-drink consumption has been directly linked to one of the highest obesity rates globally. A second cause is the increasingly sedentary nature of contemporary life. As people spend long hours seated at computers and unwind in front of screens, while children play video games rather than sport, daily energy expenditure has plummeted, allowing excess calories to accumulate as fat.\n\nTo combat this, governments should make healthy eating both affordable and attractive. Imposing a tax on sugary drinks, as the United Kingdom has done, raises prices on harmful products while funding nutrition programmes, and subsidising fruit and vegetables would encourage better choices. Schools also have a vital role: by serving balanced meals and teaching cooking and nutrition, they can instil lifelong healthy habits in children. Furthermore, urban planners could design cities with parks, cycle paths and sports facilities that make physical activity a natural part of daily routines rather than a chore.\n\nIn conclusion, obesity is driven primarily by unhealthy processed diets and inactive lifestyles. Through taxation, education and the creation of environments that promote movement and good nutrition, societies can meaningfully tackle this epidemic and improve the health of their populations."
