      "prompt": "In many countries, a large proportion of young people are unable to find work after leaving education. What are the causes of high youth unemployment, and what measures could governments take to reduce it?",
      "modelAnswer": "In numerous nations, graduates and school-leavers are entering a labour market that offers them few opportunities, leaving a generation underemployed and disillusioned. This essay will examine the causes of high youth unemployment and propose measures governments could adopt to alleviate it.\n\nOne major cause is the mismatch between the skills young people acquire and those employers actually require. Universities often produce graduates in saturated academic fields while neglecting the technical and digital competencies that growing industries demand. In Spain, for instance, thousands of humanities graduates struggle to find jobs even as technology firms report shortages of qualified programmers. A second cause is the lack of work experience that employers increasingly expect. Companies prefer candidates who can be productive immediately and are reluctant to invest in training inexperienced newcomers, creating a frustrating paradox in which young people cannot gain experience because no one will offer them a first job.\n\nGovernments can address these problems in several ways. Most importantly, they should reform education systems to align curricula with labour-market needs, expanding vocational training and apprenticeships in sectors such as engineering, healthcare and renewable energy. Germany's celebrated dual-education system, which combines classroom study with paid workplace training, has kept youth unemployment remarkably low. Additionally, governments could offer tax incentives or wage subsidies to firms that hire and train young workers, reducing the financial risk of taking on inexperienced staff and giving school-leavers a crucial foothold in the workforce.\n\nIn conclusion, youth unemployment largely results from a skills gap and the absence of early work experience. By modernising education to match economic demand and encouraging employers to recruit young people, governments can give the next generation the opportunities it deserves."
