      "modelAnswer": "Mountains of discarded plastic now choke rivers, drift across oceans and entangle marine creatures, representing one of the most visible environmental disasters of our age. This essay will consider why plastic waste keeps multiplying and outline strategies for reducing it.\n\nThe principal reason is the overwhelming prevalence of single-use plastics in modern commerce. Manufacturers favour plastic packaging because it is cheap, lightweight and durable, so everything from bottled water to takeaway meals arrives wrapped in material designed to be used once and thrown away. The sheer convenience of disposable items, such as plastic bags and coffee cups, fuels a throwaway culture that generates billions of tonnes of waste annually. A further reason is inadequate recycling infrastructure, particularly in developing countries where waste-management systems cannot cope, meaning much plastic ends up in landfills or, worse, washed into the sea, as seen in the vast garbage patches floating in the Pacific Ocean.\n\nSeveral measures could stem this tide. Governments should ban or tax the most problematic single-use plastics, as the European Union has done with straws and cutlery, prompting businesses to adopt biodegradable alternatives. Investment in modern recycling facilities and deposit-return schemes for bottles would also dramatically increase the proportion of plastic recovered and reused. Equally, corporations must be held responsible for the waste they create through extended producer-responsibility laws that force them to fund collection and recycling, encouraging redesign of packaging.\n\nIn conclusion, plastic waste proliferates because of our reliance on disposable packaging and weak recycling systems. Through bans, taxes, improved infrastructure and corporate accountability, the world can substantially curb this pollution and protect the oceans on which all life depends."
