      "modelAnswer": "Although global tourism generates substantial income and cultural exchange, it has also produced friction between visitors and residents in famous destinations. This essay will explain why such conflict arises and outline measures that could ease it.\n\nThe tension largely stems from the pressure that mass tourism places on local communities. When enormous numbers of visitors descend on a relatively small area, public spaces become overcrowded, infrastructure is overstretched and the everyday rhythm of residents is disrupted. A clear example is Venice, where the constant flow of cruise passengers clogs narrow streets and drives up prices, making the city increasingly unliveable for its dwindling population. A further source of resentment is the way short-term holiday rentals push up housing costs, forcing long-term inhabitants out of their own neighbourhoods, as has happened in Barcelona.\n\nSeveral practical measures could reduce these strains. Authorities can limit visitor numbers by introducing daily caps or timed-entry tickets to fragile sites, a policy already trialled in cities such as Dubrovnik. Imposing a tourist tax can also generate revenue to maintain local services and repair the wear caused by heavy footfall. In addition, governments should regulate holiday-rental platforms to protect housing for residents and encourage tourists to explore less-visited regions, thereby spreading the economic benefits more evenly. Promoting respectful behaviour through clear guidelines would further help visitors appreciate local customs rather than disturb them.\n\nIn conclusion, the conflict between tourists and locals arises chiefly from overcrowding and the displacement of residents, but it can be mitigated through visitor limits, taxation and sensible regulation. With careful management, popular destinations can continue to welcome travellers while safeguarding the quality of life of the people who call them home."
